course: chemistry
lesson: 28
title: Leaves, fruit and colour in nature
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In autumn you kick through piles of yellow, orange and red leaves. The yellow and orange were inside the leaf all summer, hidden under the green; when the green breaks down, they finally show.
explain: Most colours in nature are pigments, coloured chemicals that soak up some colours of light and send back the rest. A leaf's chlorophyll soaks up red and blue light and sends back green, so the leaf looks green.
explain: Food can change colour too: a tomato turns from green to red as it ripens, and a flamingo turns pink from the pink pigments in the shrimps and tiny algae it eats.
explain: Some colours are not chemicals at all. A beetle's rainbow shell, the blue sky and a rainbow are made by light bouncing, scattering and bending, not by any coloured stuff.
fact: Chlorophyll is the green pigment of leaves; it soaks up red and blue light, reflects green, and the plant uses the light's energy to make its food.
fact: In autumn the chlorophyll breaks down, and the yellow and orange pigments that were there all summer show through; red comes from new pigments some trees make in autumn.
fact: As a tomato ripens its green fades and a red pigment called lycopene builds up; tomatoes ripen faster next to bananas because bananas give off a gas called ethylene.
fact: Carrots are orange because of beta-carotene, which the body turns into vitamin A, needed for the eyes.
fact: Carrots help your eyes only if you lack vitamin A; the story that carrots gave pilots night vision is usually said to have been spread in the Second World War to hide the use of radar.
fact: Flamingos get their pink from pigments in the shrimps and algae they eat; fed without those pigments, they slowly turn white.
fact: The rainbow shine of some beetles, peacock feathers and butterfly wings comes from tiny layered structures that reflect light like a soap bubble, not from pigment.
fact: The sky is blue because the air scatters blue light much more than red light; at sunset the light passes through more air, the blue is scattered away and reds remain; with no air to scatter light, as on the Moon, the daytime sky is black.
fact: A rainbow appears when raindrops bend sunlight and split it into its colours; you see one only with the Sun behind you.
fact: Blood is always red: darker red when it carries less oxygen, bright red when it carries more; it is never blue inside the body.
fact: Veins look blue through the skin because of the way skin lets through and scatters light.
fact: Octopuses have tiny colour sacs in their skin that muscles stretch open or squeeze shut, and some can match their background in less than a second.
exercise: Why are leaves green in summer? | Chlorophyll soaks up red and blue light and reflects green | Leaves are filled with green water that shows through | Leaves reflect the colour of the grass below them | Sunlight is green in the summer
exercise: Where do the yellow and orange of autumn leaves come from? | They were there all summer, hidden under the green | Cold weather paints the leaves yellow | The leaves soak up yellow autumn sunlight and keep it | The leaves catch a yellow disease every year
exercise: Where does the red of some autumn leaves come from? | New pigments some trees make in autumn | The leaves bleeding as they slowly die off | Red dust blown from the ground | Frost turning green into red
exercise: What makes a ripening tomato turn red? | A red pigment called lycopene builds up | Sunlight paints the skin red | The seeds inside turn red first | The tomato fills with red juice from the soil
exercise: Why do tomatoes ripen faster next to bananas? | Bananas give off ethylene gas | Bananas keep the tomatoes warm | Bananas share their sugar with them | Bananas block out all the light
exercise: Why are carrots orange? | They contain beta-carotene | They soak up orange from the soil | They are dyed orange by farmers | They are full of orange vitamin C
exercise: What does the body turn beta-carotene into? | Vitamin A | Vitamin C | Iron | Sugar
exercise: Why are flamingos pink? | Pigments in the shrimps and algae they eat | They are born bright pink | They stand in pink water all day | Their red blood shows through their thin white feathers
exercise: Why does a beetle's shell shine with rainbow colours? | Tiny layers reflect light like a soap bubble | It is covered in coloured, shiny paint-like dyes | It soaks up all the colours of light | It has tiny lamps under its shell
exercise: Why is the sky blue? | Air scatters blue light more than red | The sky reflects the blue of the sea below | Space behind the air is blue | Clouds are blue on the inside
exercise: Why is the sky red at sunset? | Light passes through more air and loses its blue | The Sun gets hotter in the evening | The clouds catch fire at dusk | Red light rises from the warm sea at the end of the day
exercise: Where must the Sun be for you to see a rainbow? | Behind you | In front of you | Right above your head | Hidden behind a cloud
exercise: What colour is blood inside the body? | Always red, sometimes darker | Blue until it meets the air | Green in the veins | Clear like water
exercise: Why do veins look blue through the skin? | Skin lets through and scatters light that way | The blood inside them is blue | Veins are painted blue on the outside by the body | Veins are full of blue air
exercise: How can an octopus change colour? | Muscles open and close colour sacs in its skin | It drinks coloured sea water very quickly | It paints itself all over with ink from its body | It holds its breath until it changes
exercise: Does eating lots of carrots give you night vision? | No, they only help if you lack vitamin A | Yes, pilots learn to see in the dark this way | Yes, but only raw carrots work | No, carrots make your eyes worse
test: Lily: "leaves go yellow in autumn cos they get painted by the cold". What's true? | The yellow was there, hidden by green | She's right, cold paints the leaves | Leaves soak up yellow light in autumn | Leaves turn yellow from the rain
test: If a zoo fed its flamingos only plain food with no pink pigments, what would happen? | They would slowly turn white | They would stay pink for ever and ever | They would turn bright orange | They would turn green like leaves
test: "my veins are blue so my blood's blue inside, obvs". What's true? | Blood is always red; it only looks blue through skin | Yes, blood is blue until it meets air | Yes, blue blood is in the veins only | No, blood is actually green inside
test: Grandad says carrots let RAF pilots see in the dark. What's the real story? | That story is usually said to have hidden radar | Carrots really did give the pilots night vision | Pilots ate carrots to stay awake | Carrots were the pilots' only food
test: Jack wants to see a rainbow and looks straight at the setting Sun after a shower. Where should he look? | Away from the Sun, with it behind him | Straight into the Sun | Straight down at the puddles | Up at the very top of the sky
test: Your green tomatoes won't ripen. "stick 'em in a bag with a banana", says Nan. Why? | The banana's ethylene gas speeds ripening | The banana's sugar slowly soaks into the tomatoes | The banana keeps them in the dark | The banana's yellow rubs onto them
test: A cartoon octopus takes an hour to change colour. What's more true? | Some can change in less than a second | Octopuses cannot really change colour at all | They change only by eating coloured food | They change only when they are fast asleep
test: Which colour comes from structure, not pigment? | The rainbow shine on a peacock feather | The green of a leaf | The orange of a carrot | The red of a tomato
test: If there were no air around the Earth, what colour would the daytime sky look? | Black | Blue | Red | White
test: "why's the sunset red, the sun's on fire more at night or what?" | Its light goes through more air and loses blue | Yes, the Sun burns hotter at night than in the day | The sea reflects red light onto clouds | The Moon makes the Sun look red
test: Which is true? | Plants use the light chlorophyll soaks up to make food | Chlorophyll makes leaves green so they look nice | Chlorophyll is made only in autumn | Chlorophyll reflects red light
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the foliage of most plants appears green during summer. | Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light and reflects green light | The leaves contain a reservoir of green water | The leaves reflect the colour of surrounding vegetation | Summer sunlight consists predominantly of green light
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the pink coloration of flamingos? | It derives from pigments in their diet of shrimps and algae | It is an inherited colour present from hatching | It results from prolonged standing in pink water | It is produced by blood visible through the feathers
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the colour of human blood. | It is invariably red, varying only in shade | It is blue whilst within the veins | It becomes red only upon contact with air | It is colourless within the body
exam: It is given that an observer wishes to view a rainbow. Ascertain the position of the Sun required. | Behind the observer | Directly before the observer | Directly overhead at noon | Below the observer's horizon
